Microsoft Learn: Introduction to Azure Load Balancer

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Introduction to Azure Load Balancer
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Beginner
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn what Azure Load Balancer is, how it distributes traffic across Azure resources, and when it should be used to improve application availability, scalability, and reliability.

This module focused on understanding Azure Load Balancer functionality, its core components, how traffic distribution works, and identifying scenarios where Azure Load Balancer is the most appropriate networking solution.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Load Balancer

The module introduced Azure Load Balancer as a fully managed Azure networking service that distributes incoming and outgoing network traffic across multiple backend resources.

The primary goal of Azure Load Balancer is to ensure applications remain:

Highly available
Scalable
Fault tolerant
Responsive under heavy workloads
Example:
Users
   ↓
Azure Load Balancer
 ↓      ↓      ↓
VM1    VM2    VM3

Traffic is automatically distributed across healthy virtual machines.

Benefits:
High availability
Automatic traffic distribution
Improved application performance
Better fault tolerance
Scalability for growing workloads

The module emphasized that Azure Load Balancer helps prevent individual servers from becoming overloaded.

Step 2: Understanding Layer 4 Load Balancing

A major focus of the module was understanding where Azure Load Balancer operates within the OSI model.

Azure Load Balancer functions at:

OSI Layer 4
Transport Layer

Supported protocols:

TCP
UDP
What Layer 4 Means

Azure Load Balancer makes routing decisions based on:

Source IP address
Destination IP address
Source port
Destination port
Protocol
Important limitation:

Azure Load Balancer does not inspect:

URLs
HTTP headers
Cookies
Application content

The module highlighted that Layer 7 services such as Application Gateway are used when application-aware routing is required.

Step 3: Understanding How Azure Load Balancer Works

The training covered the primary components that make Azure Load Balancer function.

Frontend IP Configuration

The frontend receives incoming client requests.

Example:

Public IP Address
20.50.100.10

Clients connect to this address.

Backend Pool

The backend pool contains resources that receive traffic.

Examples:

Virtual Machines
Virtual Machine Scale Sets
Availability Sets

Example:

Backend Pool

VM1
VM2
VM3

The Load Balancer distributes requests among these resources.

Health Probes

Health probes continuously monitor backend resources.

Probe types:
Probe Type	Purpose
TCP	Verifies port availability
HTTP	Verifies web service health
HTTPS	Verifies secure application health

Example:

Check Port 80

If a server fails:

VM1 = Unhealthy

Traffic only sent to:
VM2
VM3
Benefits:
Automatic failover
Increased availability
Reduced downtime

The module emphasized that health probes determine whether resources are eligible to receive traffic.

Load Balancing Rules

Load balancing rules define how traffic is distributed.

Example:

Frontend Port: 80
Backend Port: 80
Protocol: TCP

These rules connect incoming traffic to backend resources.

Step 4: Public and Internal Load Balancers

The training introduced the two deployment models available in Azure.

Public Load Balancer

Distributes traffic coming from the internet.

Example:

Internet
    ↓
Public Load Balancer
    ↓
Web Servers

Common use cases:

Public websites
Internet-facing APIs
Customer applications
Internal Load Balancer

Distributes traffic within private Azure networks.

Example:

Application Servers
       ↓
Internal Load Balancer
       ↓
Database Servers

Common use cases:

Internal applications
Backend services
Multi-tier applications
Key concepts learned:
Public Load Balancers expose services externally
Internal Load Balancers support private communications only
