Step 6: When to Use Azure Load Balancer

A major focus of the module was identifying appropriate use cases.

Use Azure Load Balancer when you need:
Layer 4 load balancing
High availability
Automatic failover
TCP or UDP traffic distribution
Internal service balancing
External application balancing
Common scenarios:
Web server farms
Multi-tier applications
Virtual Machine Scale Sets
Enterprise applications
Benefits:
Improved uptime
Increased scalability
Better resource utilization
Reduced single points of failure

The module demonstrated how Azure Load Balancer supports resilient cloud architectures.

Step 7: When NOT to Use Azure Load Balancer

The module also clarified situations where other Azure services are more appropriate.

Azure Load Balancer does not provide:

SSL termination
URL-based routing
Web Application Firewall (WAF)
Cookie-based session affinity
Application-level inspection
Alternative services:
Requirement	Azure Service
URL Routing	Application Gateway
Global Traffic Distribution	Traffic Manager
Global Application Acceleration	Front Door
Web Application Firewall	Application Gateway WAF

Understanding service selection is an important AZ-104 skill.

